    {
      "abstract": "Floating offshore wind turbines (FOWTs) are often considered an expensive source of energy, and to reduce both capital and operational expenses, efforts are focused on developing lighter, more robust structures. The existing design approach for these structures typically relies on the partial safety factor (PSF) method. This method treats uncertain variables deterministically and uses PSFs to address them. As a result, this simplification often leads to designs that are either over-engineered or under-engineered. In this study, a reliability-based design optimisation (RBDO) framework for concrete FOWT support structures is developed, accurately accounting for randomness in environmental conditions and uncertainties in resistance properties. A parametric finite element analysis (FEA) model is developed based on coupled aero-hydro-structural dynamics in the time domain. The FEA results are processed using a response surface modelling technique to derive the limit state function (LSF), which is substituted into the first-order reliability method (FORM) to derive a reliability assessment model. These are further integrated with a particle swarm optimisation (PSO) algorithm to develop the RBDO framework. The deterministic design optimisation (DDO) results were compared with RBDO, revealing that DDO produces a higher mass reduction but does not predict the required reliability index. The results from the case study show that the optimised geometry achieved a maximum mass reduction of 12.77% for one of the critical failure modes, and demonstrate that the proposed RBDO framework can effectively and accurately achieve an optimal design of FOWT structures to meet target reliability.",
